ABSTRACT_EN = [
'Sensor-fault handling in automated driving stacks largely relies on a single strategy — an immediate in-lane stop — which leaves the vehicle obstructing traffic, while undetected faults let the vehicle keep driving with degraded perception. This thesis proposes a graded fault-response framework consisting of (1) a behavior-tree-based monitor that estimates per-sensor health as continuous scores from freshness, quality, and physics channels, and (2) a supervisor that selects between shoulder/parking-lot evacuation (MRM_MOVE) and deferral to the stock stop (MRM_DEFER) according to fault type, severity, and remaining capability, and enforces a conservative fixed speed cap (3.0 m/s, below the model-derived upper bound of about 3.89 m/s obtained from a stopping-distance model under assumed nominal parameters d = vτ + v²/2a) during evacuation; a health-proportional cap is implemented but was not used in the reported experiments.',
'The framework was evaluated in a closed loop of Autoware 0.39.0 and CARLA 0.9.16 (Town07, 15 km/h speed limit). A total of 1,862 valid cases — the fault-type × severity × traffic-density × method matrix together with the ablation and dose-response encounter experiments in a single closed-loop framework — verified the monitoring and evacuation behavior: the detector achieved 96-100% detection rates in every fault condition except by-design-tolerated mid LiDAR sparsification, with median detection latencies of about 1.0 s for hard fails (ledger re-measurement, simulation time) and 2.1-5.0 s for degradation faults (wall clock) with zero false activations in 45 fault-free cases, and supervision reduced degraded-state driving exposure — a representative unmonitored observation, one run per fault type — from 238-251 m to 32-58 m (detect + evacuate, camera and navigation families; LiDAR clutter 275 m to 11-12 m). Safety was quantified at the encounter level with a dual collision-verdict system — a real-time ego collision-sensor ledger (RAW-HIT) and a pedestrian-physics oracle — introduced after empirically discovering that a collision sensor attached to the pedestrian never fired; the pitfalls of runner exit labels and official infraction records are documented as a methodological contribution.',
'Distance-ladder experiments located the avoid/collide boundary at 8-11 m for 4.2 m/s driving (consistent with the model prediction of 8.8 m) and at 7-8 m for 3.0 m/s (the model value 5.79 m is a lower-bound approximation). In fault-state 8 m encounter reproductions, native continuation collided in every run while the proposed low-speed settled cruise avoided in every run (0/10 vs 8/8), and the same policy collided when encountered during the deceleration transient (2/2). The causal effect of the explicit cap was tested in a dose-response experiment restricted to settled encounters where the cap actually held (3 caps × 4 spawn distances × 10 seeds = 120 runs, pre-specified primary endpoint): at 8 m, cap 4.2 vs 3.0 m/s gave discordant pairs 10:0 (exact McNemar p=0.002; settled regime, single fault type gn-mid), and the avoid/collide boundary moved inward monotonically with lower caps (from beyond 9 m to about 7 m). The same low-speed evacuation policy launched in the deceleration transient remains at risk, and the low-speed settled cruise avoided all encounters (0/8 collisions). The MRM choice is thus a temporal trade-off between immediate collision risk (favoring the stop) and the possibility, when evacuation succeeds, of clearing the lane to an off-lane refuge position, and the residual risk of evacuation concentrates in the detection-to-deceleration transient (transient encounters collided, settled encounters did not) — an observational comparison, not a causal manipulation of detection timing, that nevertheless provides quantitative observational evidence supporting the need for earlier detection and faster deceleration to widen the safety margin within the fault-tolerant time interval (FTTI). Hard failures (complete signal loss) are decided through an evidence-aware fast path that skips noise-oriented debounce and futile recovery holds, meeting a internal 1.5-s detection-to-decision design deadline across the full cf/lf matrix (max 1.20 s, simulation-time, ledger-based). The fault model further covers correlated GNSS bias, GNSS outage, and IMU bias/drift faults, with 100% detection in all of those conditions. Limitations include the single map, the single 15 km/h speed limit, two launch corridors, and the version-specific scope of all Autoware observations.',
]
ABSTRACT_KEYWORDS = 'Keywords: autonomous vehicle, fail-safe, functional safety, minimal risk maneuver, sensor fault detection, FTTI'
